<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ladysmith, KwaZulu-Natal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ladysmith is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, on the Klip River, founded in 1850 and named after Juana María, the Spanish-born wife of the Cape governor Sir Harry Smith. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> forces besieged the British garrison there for 118 days, from November 1899 until it was relieved by Sir Redvers Buller's army on 28 February 1900 after costly battles at Colenso and Spion Kop.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ladysmith-kwazulu-natal.jpg" alt="Ladysmith, KwaZulu-Natal" width="320" height="122" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Gundelfinger · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Ladysmith_KwaZulu-Natal_South_Africa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
